  <p>Ask strength coach Jordan Hoppel for one page of advice for a new lifter and he says it would be general and boring, because that's the stuff that works.</p>

  <h2>1. Maintain consistency</h2>
  <p>Don't be ridiculously, unbelievably strict, but have a bandwidth you can stay within across life, working out, exercise, sleep, and recovery, without really high peaks and really low troughs. You don't need to be perfect all the time; manage how far you stray from the maintenance line.</p>

  <h2>2. Don't make promises you can't keep</h2>
  <p>If you're going to do something, make sure you can do it consistently. The stress and mental anguish of beating yourself up over a missed workout or eating poorly isn't worth it.</p>

  <JordanQuote>Come back the next day and do it better. You can't recover tomorrow; tomorrow's gone. You can only control right now and try to be better for the future.</JordanQuote>

  <h2>3. Don't progress too fast</h2>
  <p>Don't over-progress yourself. Beginners get rapid gains early and assume they'll keep advancing at that rate, which isn't the case.</p>

  <Media type="image" alt="Jordan Hoppel coaching a lifter on form" caption="Form first." brief="Photo: Jordan cueing a lifter at 5 Star Fitness." />

  <h2>4. Always lift with good form</h2>
  <p>Good form is the foundation of everything, and nothing should be sacrificed and put in place of it for any potential gain.</p>

  <h2>Who coaching works for</h2>
  <p>Jordan coaches best people who are willing to work hard, learn, and be open to suggestions and to trying things that may seem unconventional but are rooted in sound logic and first-principles thinking. Next, read the <a href="/beginner-lifting-guide/">beginner lifting guide</a>.</p>
</GuideLayout>
